209
00:15:33.663 --> 00:15:37.383
Specific thrust is the thrust
per unit of inlet mass flow, and

210
00:15:37.423 --> 00:15:40.779
it sets the size of the engine;
the thrust specific fuel

211
00:15:40.819 --> 00:15:44.176
consumption is the fuel flow
per unit thrust, usually in

212
00:15:44.216 --> 00:15:48.932
milligrams per second per newton, and
enters the Breguet range equation.

213
00:15:48.982 --> 00:15:53.436
The thermal efficiency is the mechanical
power produced, the increase of

214
00:15:53.476 --> 00:15:58.945
kinetic energy of all the streams, divided
by the fuel power, m dot f times h P R.

215
00:15:58.995 --> 00:16:02.626
The propulsive efficiency is
the useful power, F v zero,

216
00:16:02.666 --> 00:16:06.232
divided by that kinetic energy
increase, and the manual

217
00:16:06.272 --> 00:16:11.210
stresses that the full thrust,
including pressure terms, must be used.

218
00:16:11.260 --> 00:16:15.479
The overall efficiency is their
product, F v zero over the fuel power,

219
00:16:15.519 --> 00:16:19.616
which equals v zero over h P R times
T S F C: a perfect cross-check,

220
00:16:19.656 --> 00:16:26.126
since for exercise 1 A, 236 metres
per second over 42.5 megajoules

221
00:16:26.166 --> 00:16:33.635
times 32.49 milligrams per newton second
gives exactly the official 17.1 %.

222
00:16:35.285 --> 00:16:38.142
Here is the central trade
of the whole subject.

223
00:16:38.192 --> 00:16:41.524
For a single matched stream,
the propulsive efficiency is

224
00:16:41.564 --> 00:16:45.191
approximately two over one plus
v nine over v zero: the closer

225
00:16:45.231 --> 00:16:49.839
the jet speed to the flight speed,
the higher the propulsive efficiency.

226
00:16:49.889 --> 00:16:53.488
Raising T t four raises the
thermal efficiency but also the

227
00:16:53.528 --> 00:16:56.942
jet velocity, which costs
propulsive efficiency; raising

228
00:16:56.982 --> 00:17:00.519
the pressure ratio also raises
the thermal efficiency, but

229
00:17:00.559 --> 00:17:03.911
the jet velocity only grows
up to the pressure ratio of

230
00:17:03.951 --> 00:17:07.365
maximum specific thrust and
then falls, so beyond it the

231
00:17:07.405 --> 00:17:11.950
propulsive efficiency recovers, as
the design-trend slide will show.

232
00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:15.247
The turbofan breaks the coupling:
it takes the core power and

233
00:17:15.287 --> 00:17:18.642
spreads it over a much larger
mass flow at a lower velocity, so

234
00:17:18.682 --> 00:17:21.983
the overall efficiency improves
even if the thermal efficiency

235
00:17:22.023 --> 00:17:25.875
does not, and the propeller is the
extreme case of the same idea.

236
00:17:25.925 --> 00:17:28.941
That is the reason bypass
ratios have kept growing

237
00:17:28.981 --> 00:17:32.754
for four decades, and why open
rotors are studied today.

238
00:17:34.404 --> 00:17:36.565
All of this assembles into an explicit

239
00:17:36.605 --> 00:17:40.141
procedure with no iteration,
the same for every engine.

240
00:17:40.191 --> 00:17:44.836
Step one, gas properties and flight
condition; step two, the inlet.

241
00:17:44.886 --> 00:17:48.254
Step three, the compressor
temperature ratio, and

242
00:17:48.294 --> 00:17:52.469
step four, the burner: tau
lambda and the fuel ratio f.

243
00:17:52.519 --> 00:17:56.146
Step five, the turbine from
the power balance, first tau

244
00:17:56.186 --> 00:18:00.334
t, then pi t; this is the step
students most often forget.

245
00:18:00.384 --> 00:18:04.821
Step six, the nozzle: the total-to-static
pressure ratio is the product of

246
00:18:04.861 --> 00:18:09.298
all the pi's times P zero over P nine,
then the exit Mach number, with the

247
00:18:09.338 --> 00:18:13.472
choked or matched check for a
convergent nozzle; step seven, the exit

248
00:18:13.512 --> 00:18:18.823
temperature and velocity, being careful
with the different gamma R of the hot gas.

249
00:18:18.873 --> 00:18:22.919
Step eight, the performance:
specific thrust, T S F C equal

250
00:18:22.959 --> 00:18:27.327
to f over the specific thrust,
and the three efficiencies.

251
00:18:28.977 --> 00:18:33.676
The manual's worked example, from
Mattingly, lets you validate the procedure

252
00:18:33.716 --> 00:18:40.419
line by line: Mach 2 at 12 km, compressor
pressure ratio 10, T t four 1 800

253
00:18:40.459 --> 00:18:45.488
kelvin and a convergent-divergent nozzle
with P zero over P nine of one half.

254
00:18:45.538 --> 00:18:50.298
The free-stream gives tau
r 1.8 and pi r 7.82, and

255
00:18:50.338 --> 00:18:56.279
the shock recovery 0.925
brings pi d down to 0.879.

256
00:18:56.329 --> 00:19:02.752
The compressor gives tau c 2.08 with
an isentropic efficiency of 0.864,

257
00:19:02.792 --> 00:19:10.088
below the polytropic 0.9 as expected,
and the burner needs f equal to 0.0357.

258
00:19:10.138 --> 00:19:17.139
The power balance gives tau t 0.8155
and pi t 0.375, the nozzle expands to

259
00:19:17.179 --> 00:19:23.631
Mach 2.25, and the jet leaves at 4.25
times the free-stream speed of sound.

260
00:19:23.681 --> 00:19:28.846
The specific thrust is 807 newton
seconds per kilogram and the T S F C

261
00:19:28.886 --> 00:19:36.349
44.2 milligrams per second per newton,
with efficiencies of 42, 74 and 31 %.

262
00:19:36.399 --> 00:19:40.297
The propulsive efficiency is high
because at Mach 2 the jet is only

263
00:19:40.337 --> 00:19:44.121
about twice as fast as the aircraft,
and the strong ram compression

264
00:19:44.161 --> 00:19:48.002
keeps the thermal efficiency high
despite the inlet shock loss; that

265
00:19:48.042 --> 00:19:52.387
is why turbojets survive at high Mach
numbers, where turbofans do not.

266
00:19:54.037 --> 00:19:57.499
Two trends must be
understood qualitatively.

267
00:19:57.549 --> 00:20:01.424
There is an optimum compressor
pressure ratio: raising it raises the

268
00:20:01.464 --> 00:20:05.454
thermal efficiency, but the turbine
must then expand more to drive it,

269
00:20:05.494 --> 00:20:10.565
leaving less pressure for the nozzle, so
beyond a point the specific thrust falls.

270
00:20:10.615 --> 00:20:14.525
For the ideal cycle, maximum
specific thrust occurs when tau c

271
00:20:14.565 --> 00:20:18.602
equals the square root of tau
lambda divided by tau r, while the

272
00:20:18.642 --> 00:20:22.678
minimum fuel consumption occurs
at a much higher pressure ratio,

273
00:20:22.718 --> 00:20:27.719
so real engines are designed between
the two, as these ideal curves show.

274
00:20:27.769 --> 00:20:31.593
And as the flight Mach number
grows, the ram compression does the

275
00:20:31.633 --> 00:20:35.756
job and the optimum pressure ratio
falls, until above Mach 3 or so

276
00:20:35.796 --> 00:20:40.367
the compressor and turbine can be
removed: the engine becomes a ramjet.

277
00:20:42.017 --> 00:20:45.931
Let me summarise part one as the
first page of your cheat sheet.

278
00:20:45.981 --> 00:20:49.247
Total temperature is conserved
without work or heat,

279
00:20:49.287 --> 00:20:52.744
total pressure falls with
losses, and each component is

280
00:20:52.784 --> 00:20:56.885
a tau and a pi, with tau lambda
as the key cycle parameter.

281
00:20:56.935 --> 00:20:59.639
Write both forms of the
compressor and turbine

282
00:20:59.679 --> 00:21:02.741
relations, polytropic and
isentropic, the fuel ratio

283
00:21:02.781 --> 00:21:06.770
formula, and the reminder that
the power balance gives tau t.

284
00:21:06.820 --> 00:21:10.061
Write the convergent nozzle
check with its two critical

285
00:21:10.101 --> 00:21:14.090
ratios, and the mass flow parameter
formula for sizing areas.

286
00:21:14.140 --> 00:21:18.687
And the thrust, T S F C and efficiency
formulas, including the quick

287
00:21:18.727 --> 00:21:23.731
check that the overall efficiency
is v zero over h P R times T S F C.

288
00:21:23.781 --> 00:21:27.728
In part two we apply all of it to
the turbofan of exercise 1 A, to

289
00:21:27.768 --> 00:21:32.340
its static version 1 B, and to the
propfan and open rotor conversions.
